The '''2024 Portblanc fires''' were a series of {{w|grass fire|grass fires}} that burned through the northwestern districts of [[Portblanc]], the capital of [[Tramuntana Province]], [[Balboa]], from 14 to 17 March 2024. The fires destroyed approximately 1,200 houses and caused eight deaths, making them the most destructive grass fire event in the city's history. The fires were ignited by leftover burns from a {{w|gender reveal party}} held on a hillside property in the northwestern districts, where spent pyrotechnic material reignited in the dry conditions and set fire to the surrounding grassland. The fires occurred during the dry season, when the dry vegetation on the hillsides of the coastal Tramuntana range — the small elevated range that runs along the Tramuntana coast — became susceptible to ignition after an extended period of below-average rainfall.

== Background ==

Portblanc has an unusually dry {{w|tropical savanna climate}} for the Shendan coastal region, with annual rainfall averaging only 800–1,000 mm. The dryness is a consequence of the double rain shadow produced by the coastal Tramuntana range, which intercepts moisture-bearing winds from the [[Shendan Ocean]], and the main [[Yalabirga Mountain Range|Yalabirga]] highlands to the east, which block humid air masses from the interior. The dry season lasts from December to April, during which the grasslands on the hillsides surrounding the city dry out and become susceptible to fire.

In the decades preceding the fires, residential development had expanded from the coastal plain into the hills of the coastal Tramuntana range, particularly in the northwestern districts of the city. The expansion of housing into areas of dry grassland increased the interface between residential properties and fire-prone vegetation, a pattern that had been identified as a risk by provincial planning authorities but had not been accompanied by adequate firebreaks or building codes addressing wildfire risk.

The drying trend associated with {{w|climate change}} had exacerbated the fire risk in the region. Rainfall in the 2023–2024 dry season was significantly below average, and by March 2024, the vegetation on the hillsides was at its driest recorded level.

== Fires ==

The fires began on 14 March 2024, when a grass fire ignited on the upper slopes of the coastal Tramuntana range in the northwestern districts of Portblanc. The ignition was traced to leftover burns from a {{w|gender reveal party}} held on a hillside property two days earlier, on 12 March. The party had used pyrotechnic devices that produced coloured smoke — blue or pink — to announce the sex of an expected child, and the spent material, not fully extinguished, reignited in the dry conditions and set fire to the surrounding grassland. The discovery of the cause, made public in the course of the subsequent investigation, attracted significant attention and controversy, both for the recklessness of the ignition and for the severity of the consequences that followed from it.

Strong winds from the Shendan Ocean carried the fire rapidly downslope toward residential areas, and the fire spread through the dry grasslands faster than emergency services could contain it.

By the morning of 15 March, the fire had reached the residential neighbourhoods on the northwestern fringe of the city, where housing had been built in close proximity to dry grassland without adequate firebreaks. The fire advanced through the neighbourhoods, destroying houses in rapid succession. Emergency services evacuated the affected districts, but the speed of the fire's advance and the limited access roads in the hillside neighbourhoods impeded the evacuation.

A second fire ignited on 15 March in a separate area of the northwestern hills, approximately three kilometres from the first fire. The two fires burned concurrently over the following two days, fed by continued dry winds and the abundance of dry vegetation. The fires were brought under control on 17 March, after the winds subsided and firefighting resources from the [[Portblanc Military Academy]] and neighbouring provinces were deployed to assist the city's fire service.

== Casualties and damage ==

The fires destroyed approximately 1,200 houses, the majority in the northwestern residential districts that had expanded into the hills of the coastal Tramuntana range. Eight deaths were confirmed, all residents of the affected neighbourhoods who were unable to evacuate before the fire reached their homes. Several dozen injuries were reported among residents and emergency responders.

The destruction was concentrated in the hillside neighbourhoods, where houses had been built in close proximity to dry grassland without adequate firebreaks or fire-resistant construction. The limited road access in the hillside districts impeded both evacuation and firefighting efforts, contributing to the severity of the damage.

== Aftermath ==

The 2024 fires prompted a review of land use and building codes in the hillside areas of Portblanc. The Tramuntana Provincial Assembly passed legislation in late 2024 requiring firebreaks between residential developments and grassland in the coastal Tramuntana range, mandating fire-resistant building materials for new construction in designated fire-risk zones, and improving road access for emergency vehicles in hillside neighbourhoods.

The fires also intensified the national debate over the impact of {{w|climate change}} on Balboa's fire risk, particularly in the drier provinces of Tramuntana and Nurra. Environmental organisations and the [[Socialist Front of Balboa]] called for accelerated action under the [[1996 Adonian Community Edict on a Green Transition for the New Millennium]], arguing that the drying trend in the northern provinces required a coordinated response from the national government. The Tramuntana Regionalist Party cited the fires as evidence of the province's neglect by the Estret- and Jala-centric national government, arguing that inadequate infrastructure and emergency services in the province had worsened the outcome.

Reconstruction of the affected neighbourhoods began in 2025, though progress was slow due to the province's limited fiscal capacity and the competing demands on the national disaster relief budget. Some displaced residents were relocated to new housing on the coastal plain, away from the fire-prone hillsides, while others chose to rebuild on their original lots under the new building codes.

